---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Why Is My Car Battery Draining? The Parasitic Draw Diagnosis';
const description = 'A car battery that goes flat overnight usually has a parasitic draw — something staying on after the key leaves. The diagnosis path from drain to culprit.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/car-battery-draining/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Car battery draining',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why does my car battery keep dying overnight?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Usually a parasitic draw — something staying powered after the key is out (lights, alarm, infotainment, a stuck relay). Normal draw is tens of milliamps; a flat battery overnight means something is drawing far more, or the battery itself can no longer hold charge.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How do you find a parasitic battery drain?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Measure the sleep current with an ammeter (normal is roughly 20-50 mA), then pull fuses one by one — the fuse whose removal drops the current is the circuit hiding the drain. Then trace that circuit to the culprit.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Problems · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Why Is My Car Battery Draining? The Parasitic Draw Diagnosis</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        When a healthy battery goes flat overnight, the battery is usually innocent — the car is awake while you
        sleep, and the diagnosis is finding which circuit refused to shut down.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A car battery that drains overnight usually has a <strong>parasitic
        draw</strong> — something staying powered after the key is out: lights, alarm, infotainment, a stuck relay.
        Normal sleep current is roughly <strong>20–50 mA</strong>; a flat battery by morning means far more is
        flowing, or the battery can no longer hold what
        <a href="/lead-acid/self-discharge/">self-discharge</a> asks of it.
      </div>

      <h2>The diagnosis path</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1. Verify the battery</strong></td><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/cca-testing/">Test it</a> — a weak battery mimics a drain</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2. Measure sleep current</strong></td><td>Ammeter in series — normal ~20–50 mA after shutdown</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3. Pull fuses</strong></td><td>One by one — the fuse that drops the current marks the circuit</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>4. Trace the circuit</strong></td><td>Lights, aftermarket devices, relays are the usual culprits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The usual culprits</h2>
      <p>
        Aftermarket accessories lead the list — dashcams on constant power, alarms, phone chargers left plugged
        in — followed by interior lights, trunk lights with failed switches, and modules that never enter sleep.
        The pattern is constant: the draw hides in what was <em>added</em> or what <em>fails to switch off</em>,
        rarely in the factory design itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Drain vs a dying battery</h2>
      <p>
        The two confuse each other. A battery that has lost capacity (age, heat, deep discharges) goes flat from a
        <em>normal</em> draw, while a healthy battery goes flat from an <em>abnormal</em> one. The test separates
        them: verify the battery first, then measure the draw — the order that prevents replacing a good battery
        for a circuit's sin (see <a href="/lead-acid/how-long-does-a-car-battery-last/">lifespan</a>).
      </p>
      <h2>The typical draws</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Load</th><th>Typical draw</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Factory sleep (modules off)</td><td>~20–50 mA — normal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dashcam on constant power</td><td>~100–300 mA — a slow drain</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alarm / telematics standby</td><td>~20–80 mA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Interior light left on</td><td>~500 mA–1 A+ — flattens overnight</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Typical order-of-magnitude values — the diagnosis compares the measured sleep current against the
        ~20–50 mA baseline.
      </p>


      <h2>The Editor's View</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My read:</strong> The overnight-flat mystery is almost always a measurement problem solved in
          order — battery first, current second, fuses third — and the discipline of that order saves more
          misdiagnosed batteries than any tool.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> Drain and battery failure produce identical symptoms through opposite causes.
          Testing the battery before chasing the circuit avoids the expensive classic — a new battery installed to
          fix a draw that empties it within the month.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Why does my car battery keep dying overnight?</h3>
      <p>Usually a parasitic draw — something staying powered after the key is out (lights, alarm, infotainment, a stuck relay). Normal draw is tens of milliamps; a flat battery overnight means something is drawing far more, or the battery itself can no longer hold charge.</p>
      <h3>How do you find a parasitic battery drain?</h3>
      <p>Measure the sleep current with an ammeter (normal is roughly 20-50 mA), then pull fuses one by one — the fuse whose removal drops the current is the circuit hiding the drain. Then trace that circuit to the culprit.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from automotive electrical references and BCI guidance.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automotive electrical references — parasitic draw measurement and diagnosis.</li>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — battery testing and application guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
